[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://twitter.com/thsottiaux/status/2125784147889393806", :title "OpenAI cuts GPT-6.2 usage: Astra hits limits 40% faster, Sol 25% faster", :link "https://twitter.com/thsottiaux/status/2125784147889393806", :published-at #inst "2026-11-26T03:36:58.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "OpenAI cuts GPT-6.2 usage: Astra hits limits 40% faster, Sol 25%...", :matched-terms ["gpt"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn-frontpage", :item-id "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50648365", :title "Bare Metal Qwen", :link "https://github.com/alesha-pro/llm.asm/tree/main/02-baremetal-os", :published-at #inst "2026-11-25T22:15:34.000-00:00", :content "Article URL: https://github.com/alesha-pro/llm.asm/tree/main/02-baremetal-os Comments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50648365 Points: 134 # Comments: 30"}, :excerpts [{:text "Article URL: https://github.com/alesha-pro/llm.asm/tree/main/02-baremetal-os Comments URL:...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://datahike.io/notes/postgres-data-in-datahike-without-migrating/", :title "Bring PostgreSQL data into Datahike without migrating", :link "https://datahike.io/notes/postgres-data-in-datahike-without-migrating/", :published-at #inst "2026-11-25T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "Bring PostgreSQL data into Datahike without migrating November 2026 Most of what we have written about Datahike assumes you are willing to put data into it: history on every table, branches you can query, a database that runs in the browser. If your data lives in PostgreSQL and your application writes to PostgreSQL, those were reasons to consider a migration, and a migration is a large price for trying an idea. pg-datahike is the adapter that lets PostgreSQL clients talk to Datahike, and it ships inside Datahike Server. With pg-datahike 0.1.428, bundled in Datahike Server 0.8.1951, that price is gone for reads. The server can subscribe to a PostgreSQL 18 publication and keep a continuously updated copy in Datahike, using the logical replication protocol PostgreSQL already speaks. The application and its writes stay where they are. What you gain is a second database, fed by the first, that has Datahike’s properties. A subscriber, as PostgreSQL understands it On the PostgreSQL side the mirror looks like any logical replication subscriber. It needs wal_level = logical, a publication, and a login with the REPLICATION attribute. There is no extension to install and no trigger on your tables. CREATE PUBLICATION analytics FOR TABLE accounts, events;\n\nCREATE ROLE mirror LOGIN REPLICATION;\nGRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE production TO mirror;\nGRANT SELECT ON accounts, events TO mirror;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION pg_catalog.pg_logical_emit_message(boolean, text, text, boolean) TO mirror; On the other side, one Datahike Server process runs in mirror mode. It owns a Datahike store and a replication slot, and it needs a three-line configuration: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? ;; mirror.edn\n{:publication \"analytics\", :store-path \"/var/lib/datahike/mirrors/analytics\",\n :server {:host \"127.0.0.1\", :port 15432, :default \"analytics\"}} ;; mirror.edn\n{:publication \"analytics\"\n :store-path \"/var/lib/datahike/mirrors/analytics\"\n :server {:host \"127.0.0.1\" :port 15432 :default \"analytics\"}} export PG_MIRROR_SOURCE_URL='jdbc:postgresql://publisher.example/production?user=mirror&sslmode=verify-full'\njava -jar datahike-http-server-0.8.1951-standalone.jar mirror --check mirror.edn\njava -jar datahike-http-server-0.8.1951-standalone.jar mirror mirror.edn The JAR is on the Datahike releases page, and the same build is published as the container image ghcr.io/replikativ/datahike-server. For containers, the mirror is one more service next to the HTTP server, started with the mirror command and its own volume; the server guide has a Docker Compose file for that arrangement, with credentials in secret files. If you already run Datahike Server from a shared server.edn, the mirror can live there instead of in its own file. Each entry under :pg-mirrors is a named mirror with the same options, and the source URL comes from a secret file that only the worker’s process or container can read: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? ;; server.edn\n{:pg-mirrors {\"analytics\" {:publication \"analytics\",\n                           :source-url-file \"/run/secrets/analytics-jdbc-url\",\n                           :store-path \"/var/lib/datahike/mirrors/analytics\",\n                           :server {:host \"127.0.0.1\", :port 15432}}}} ;; server.edn\n{:pg-mirrors\n {\"analytics\" {:publication \"analytics\"\n               :source-url-file \"/run/secrets/analytics-jdbc-url\"\n               :store-path \"/var/lib/datahike/mirrors/analytics\"\n               :server {:host \"127.0.0.1\" :port 15432}}}} java -jar datahike-http-server-0.8.1951-standalone.jar mirror --check --name analytics --config server.edn\njava -jar datahike-http-server-0.8.1951-standalone.jar mirror --name analytics --config server.edn Either way the mirror runs as its own process, started by your supervisor or as a separate Compose service. The HTTP server reads the same file and leaves the entry alone. A named mirror’s name becomes its SQL database name. The --check run inspects the publication and reports, column by column, anything the mirror cannot take, before it copies a single row. The real run starts by creating a replication slot. PostgreSQL answers with a snapshot that corresponds to an exact position in its write-ahead log (WAL), and the mirror copies the published tables inside that snapshot. It then asks for the stream of changes from the same position, so a write that commits during the copy arrives through the stream and nothing is missed or applied twice. The copy and the position it belongs to are stored in one local transaction. Once the mirror has caught up to the present, it opens its SQL port. From then on PostgreSQL does the work it always did: it runs the application’s SQL, its triggers and its cascades. The mirror receives the resulting inserts, updates and deletes, grouped by the transaction that made them. Because this is logical replication, it builds its own indexes from those rows. It is a read model, and it cannot be promoted to replace the primary. What changes on the primary wal_level is a cluster-wide setting, and changing it needs a restart. At logical, PostgreSQL writes somewhat more WAL, mostly for updates and deletes on tables set to REPLICA IDENTITY FULL, and it spends processor time decoding changes for the mirror. The mirror also writes to the primary in one small way: it emits logical messages as markers, which is what the pg_logical_emit_message grant is for. The setting to think about beforehand is WAL retention. A replication slot makes the primary keep the WAL its subscriber has not confirmed. That is what lets a mirror resume after an outage, and it is also how a stopped or failed mirror fills a disk. A mirror whose first snapshot fails drops its slot if it can still reach the primary, but one that is killed or cut off mid-copy leaves the slot behind until it is started again. Set max_slot_wal_keep_size on the primary so that a forgotten mirror loses its slot before production loses its disk. The first thing you get: the past A PostgreSQL table holds the current row. The mirror applies every publisher commit as its own Datahike transaction, and Datahike keeps what a transaction replaces. So once the mirror is running, each table has a history that nobody had to design, and the mirror’s PostgreSQL-compatible listener lets you read it with the client you already use: $ psql --host=127.0.0.1 --port=15432 --username=datahike --dbname=analytics\nanalytics=> SELECT status FROM accounts WHERE id = 4218;\n  status\n-----------\n suspended\n\nanalytics=> SET datahike.as_of = '2026-11-18T09:00:00Z';\nSET\nanalytics=> SELECT status FROM accounts WHERE id = 4218;\n status\n--------\n active Time in that history is PostgreSQL’s. The mirror records each transaction at the commit timestamp the primary gave it, to the millisecond, so as_of returns what the primary had committed at that moment, however far behind the mirror was when it applied the change. The history starts at the snapshot. Changes made before the mirror first connected were never sent to it, and no setting brings them back. Datahike speaks PostgreSQL describes the SQL surface in general; on a mirror it is read-only, and a write returns the same error PostgreSQL gives inside a read-only transaction. The second: it is a Datahike database The SQL listener is one way in. Underneath it, the mirror’s store is an ordinary Datahike database. A mirrored table is a set of attributes, a row is an entity, and the same data answers a Datalog query: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? d/q('[:find ?id ?status\n      :where [?a :accounts/id ?id]\n             [?a :accounts/status ?status]],\n  db) (d/q '[:find ?id ?status\n       :where [?a :accounts/id ?id]\n              [?a :accounts/status ?status]]\n     db) That matters because of what Datahike already does with a database once it has one. It is reachable from more than SQL. Datahike’s API is specified once and generated for Clojure, Java, JavaScript and TypeScript, Python, C and a command-line tool. Each of them can query a database value, including a past one, and join it with other Datahike databases in one query. It can be served to browsers. Datahike Server exposes a database over HTTP. A page can use the 5 KB thin client and let the server run the queries, or it can hold a replica that receives the database’s index blocks, stores them in IndexedDB, and answers queries locally, including while offline. What comes after InstantDB? walks through those modes and their trade-offs. With a mirror in front, the data such a page reads can be data your existing PostgreSQL application writes. Readers do not load the writer. Datahike stores its indexes as immutable blocks, and a reader fetches the blocks it needs from storage directly. Adding readers adds no work to the process that follows PostgreSQL, and none to PostgreSQL itself, which sees one replication connection however many people query the copy. One boundary belongs here. Today the mirror process serves its store over the SQL port, and that is what we test. Datahike Server’s HTTP API runs as a separate process with its own databases. Reading the mirror’s store through that API or another binding relies on Datahike’s ordinary behaviour for readers of a store that somebody else writes, and we have not yet published a tested recipe for that combination. If it is the reason you are interested, tell us, because it decides what we document next. What survives a failure The mirror records its replication position in the same Datahike transaction as the rows of the publisher commit that position belongs to. A crash loses both or neither, so on restart the mirror reads its position from its own store, skips what it already has, and continues. It confirms a position to PostgreSQL only once the matching local commit is on disk. One of our tests kills the mirror process in the middle of a backlog, with and without the queue described below, and checks after the restart that every publisher transaction is in the store exactly once. The usual consequence of a replication slot applies: while the mirror is stopped or behind, the primary retains the write-ahead log the mirror still needs. The mirror’s status reports how much. If the slot is dropped or invalidated, the mirror pauses and keeps serving its last committed state until an operator takes a new snapshot. Throughput A mirror has to finish each transaction durably before it may confirm it, so its ceiling is set by how fast it can commit to its own disk. By default it handles one publisher commit at a time. Version 0.1.427 added an experimental setting, :max-in-flight-commits, that lets it queue up to 32 publisher commits in the Datahike writer. The writer applies them in source order, each as its own transaction and its own point in history, and writes several of them to storage in one physical commit. We measured this on 25 November 2026, on one laptop (Intel Core Ultra 7 258V, Ubuntu 26.04), with the source that was released as 0.1.427. The mirror ran on OpenJDK 21, limited to four processors and a 2 GB heap, with its store on ZFS and a sync on every write. The PostgreSQL 18.6 publisher kept its data on tmpfs, so that it would not compete for the disk. One client inserted one small row per transaction at a fixed rate. Lag is the time from the publisher’s commit timestamp to the durable commit on the mirror. Offered rate Runs at depth 32 Applied during the run p95 lag 65 tx/s 1 × 120 s 65 tx/s 0.48 s 100 tx/s 2 × 30 s 99 and 100 tx/s 0.89 and 1.84 s 150 tx/s 2 × 30 s 149 and 104 tx/s 0.16 and 9.5 s 250 tx/s 2 × 30 s 181 and 149 tx/s 8.5 and 15.8 s In every run the mirror worked off its backlog after the load stopped, and a digest over all rows matched the publisher’s. For scale, we ran the same single-row inserts against PostgreSQL itself, unpaced, with its data on the same ZFS pool and durable commits. One client reached between 290 and 671 transactions per second. Both systems are waiting for the same disk to confirm a write, and on that disk the mirror followed roughly a quarter to a half of what PostgreSQL could commit from one connection. A primary with many concurrent clients commits far more than that, and this mirror would not keep up with it. Two things limit how far these numbers reach. The runs are short and few, and they vary: at 150 transactions per second one run stayed 0.16 seconds behind and the other fell 9.5 seconds behind. And the queue helps most when transactions are small. With seven row changes per transaction, offered at 100 transactions per second, the mirror applied between 15 and 24 per second at depth 32, and a deeper queue did not change that. The cost there is in applying the rows of one commit, which is what we are profiling now. We did not measure the default depth of one in this series; at depth 8 the mirror already fell behind at 65 transactions per second. So the setting is worth trying if your mirror lags and your publisher commits many small transactions: Superficie Clojure What is this syntax? {:publication \"analytics\", :store-path \"/var/lib/datahike/mirrors/analytics\",\n :max-in-flight-commits 32,\n :server {:host \"127.0.0.1\", :port 15432, :default \"analytics\"}} {:publication \"analytics\"\n :store-path \"/var/lib/datahike/mirrors/analytics\"\n :max-in-flight-commits 32\n :server {:host \"127.0.0.1\" :port 15432 :default \"analytics\"}} In a server.edn, the same key goes into the mirror’s entry under :pg-mirrors. The queue is labelled experimental, which here means the option and its limits may change between releases. It also requires that the mirror be the only Datahike process writing to its store. Not every commit is eligible. The queue takes plain inserts, updates and deletes on tables with the default replica identity and without enum, domain or generated columns. Everything else, including TRUNCATE, schema changes and large streamed transactions, waits for the queue to empty and is applied one at a time. Limitations The mirror follows PostgreSQL 18 publishers. It takes whole, unfiltered, permanent tables that have a replica identity: a primary key, a suitable unique index, or REPLICA IDENTITY FULL. Row filters, partial column lists, sequence counters and virtual generated columns are not mirrored, and partitioned tables follow their own rules. The supported column types are the common scalar types and their arrays, json, jsonb, tsvector, enums, and domains over supported types. You do not need to memorize this list, since --check applies it to your publication and names what fails. The limit most likely to slow a first attempt is the initial snapshot. Since 0.1.428 the mirror commits it in batches of about 16 MiB, so the copy no longer has to fit in memory and has no size limit. It is slow, though. In one run on the laptop described above, with a 768 MB heap, a table of 600,000 rows (62 MB of COPY data) took 204 seconds, about 3,000 rows per second. A snapshot that is interrupted starts over from the beginning. Once the copy is done, catching up may take as long as it needs: the mirror only reconnects if nothing arrives for ten minutes. Two cautions apply while the snapshot runs. Do not TRUNCATE a published table, because PostgreSQL can then hand the mirror an empty table and its history will be wrong from the start. And a very large table with a composite primary key is still checked for uniqueness in memory. Schema changes on the publisher pause the mirror unless :schema-refresh? is enabled. With it, the mirror takes a fresh snapshot of the affected tables in the background and swaps it in at a WAL position, and under write load that swap is where we have seen the longest delays. Types outside the supported list, such as interval, inet, ranges, composite types and extension types, are rejected by the check. The mirror’s store keeps every version and has no retention policy yet, so it grows with every commit. Defaults, triggers, cascades and constraints stay PostgreSQL’s business. The mirror records the rows they produce. It accepts no writes, it is not a failover target, and a single publisher transaction above the configured size limit (64 MiB by default) pauses it until an operator raises the limit. pg-datahike as a whole is beta software, and the mirror is its newest part. Try it Download the Datahike Server JAR from the Datahike releases or pull ghcr.io/replikativ/datahike-server, create a publication on a PostgreSQL 18 test database, and run mirror --check against it. The mirroring guide covers publications, schema changes on the publisher, recovery and status. If you only want the adapter, or you embed it in your own JVM service, the pg-datahike standalone JAR accepts the same mirror command and configuration, and datahike.pg.mirror/start! starts a mirror from Clojure. If the check rejects your publication, or the mirror cannot follow your write rate, an issue with the report is the most useful thing you can send us."}, :excerpts [{:text "... updated copy in Datahike, using the logical replication protocol PostgreSQL already speaks. The...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content} {:text "... side the mirror looks like any logical replication subscriber. It needs wal_level = logical, a publication, and a login with the REPLICATION attribute. There is no extension to...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content} {:text "... accounts, events;\n\nCREATE ROLE mirror LOGIN REPLICATION;\nGRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE production TO...", :matched-terms ["repl"], :source :content}]}]